{
 "id": "2020-portnov-street",
 "name": "Street Lighting and Feeling Safe After Dark",
 "description": "Rebuilt from the published description of Portnov, Saad, Trop, Kliger & Svechkina (2020). Observers walk a set route after dark and, at each numbered point, rate four lighting attributes (illumination, light colour temperature, uniformity, glare), their feeling of safety and overall lighting quality, each on the paper's 0–3 scale. Each report saves the route, point and time; the paper related feeling of safety to the lighting attributes with ordered logistic regression, controlling for person, place and time. In the paper, 380 observers made about 26,000 reports at 257 points in Tel Aviv-Yafo, Haifa and Beersheba. Differences from the original study: (1) The paper used its own phone app (CityLights), which checked by GPS that each report was made at the designated point and only after dark; this page cannot use GPS, so observers choose the point number themselves and the time is recorded for each report. (2) Route maps and point lists come from the researcher (give each observer a route code); the paper's routes were 800–1000 m with 20–30 points about 20–30 m apart. (3) The scale labels are the paper's; the question wording is written for this template, since the paper does not print it. (4) Vegetation density and traffic intensity were assessed by the researchers per route and are not part of this page.",
